a head knows whether it has every row the daemon says it has

R17. Measured from outside this head on 2026-09-22, twice, and the second one is
the argument:

* Three prompts rendered `queued ·` on the screen while the tree was clean, no turn
  was running, and the work they asked for was already committed. (**R16**, the
  echo that outlives its fact — the same root cause, and it is next.)
* At 22:52 this head's last row was a prompt at **seq 339** and the ledger held
  **seq 375**: thirty-six rows of this head's own work, and **the head did not think
  it was scrolled back** — there was no scrolled-back seam, so it believed it was at
  the tail. Every judgement anybody makes from that screen is unsafe, including the
  operator's about whether to send the next prompt, which they got wrong three times
  in one night for exactly this reason.

**So the requirement is not "a row can go missing". It is that nothing tells anyone
it did.** `seq` is the log's position, dense, and assigned by the daemon alone — and
this head assigned it unconditionally (`self.seq = env.seq`), which is precisely what
makes a delivered row and a dropped one look the same. Three numbers, because the
same screen is produced by three different faults in three different places.

## `gaps` — the wire lost them

`env.seq > self.seq + 1` is proof, not a guess. Counted, **said** in the conversation
naming the range (`seq 2..3 are missing`), and **repaired** by asking for the resync
that exists for exactly this. Counted and not merely repaired because a gap repaired
in silence looks identical to a session that never had one — and then nobody learns
that a socket, a queue or a compaction is losing rows.

Guarded on the session, so a `Switch` — whose events can arrive on the same socket
before the new `Hello` has been folded — cannot read the other session's numbering as
a gap in this one's. And not on `self.seq == 0`, which is a head with no mark yet.

## `behind` — the daemon still has them

This is the state that was measured and could not be named. A head that is *behind*
has an **empty queue and a correct screen**: it has drawn everything it was given and
there is nothing more coming yet. From inside, that is indistinguishable from
*current* — which is why the screen said nothing, and why the operator read a working
agent as an idle one. A head cannot see the daemon's position unless the daemon states
it, and there is already one frame that does so without being asked: an `Accepted`
carries the seq at which the command's effect is visible, and a `Rejected` carries the
seq the daemon is actually at. Either is the daemon saying *"I am here"*; compared
with `self.seq` it is a distance, and a lower bound is enough to say *"not current"*.

Deliberately not an alarm: an ordinary backlog is not a fault, so it is on `/status`
and not on the border.

## `orphan_bodies` — the wire delivered them and there is no row to put them on

**Found by reading the two ends rather than guessing at one.** `App::record_item`
ended `let Some(idx) = … else { return; }` — a body whose id is not in `items` was
thrown away **in silence**, and there is not even a placeholder left to notice,
because a body-less row renders as `Vec::new()`. That is a third way a row the ledger
has can be absent from the screen, and it was invisible in a way the other two are
not: for a lost event there is a jump in `seq` to look for, and for a missing body
there is a row on screen, but for this there is nothing at all.

Counted, and said with the id, because the words cannot be drawn — the row is gone.
Its one known cause is not a bug in this head (a snapshot replaces `items` wholesale,
and the daemon's view is bounded at 2000 rows / 8 MB of bodies, so a body for a row
the snapshot already trimmed has nowhere to go); what can be wrong is the
*frequency*, and a number is how that becomes visible.

## What I could not settle, and the instrument that settles it

The operator asked for the boring explanation to be ruled out first: did those rows
**arrive and not get drawn**, or **never arrive**? I cannot answer that for the
22:52 window from outside, and I will not pretend to: the three candidate causes were
all live and only the head's own numbers separate them. What I *can* say from reading
both ends is what each looks like, and that is now on `/status`:

| | `gaps` | `behind` | `orphan` |
|---|---|---|---|
| **the wire lost them** | > 0 | — | — |
| **not here yet** (hub queue, socket, this head's channel) | 0 | > 0 | — |
| **delivered, no row to land on** | 0 | — | > 0 |

`gaps == 0 && behind == 0 && orphan == 0` while the ledger is ahead is the one state
these three cannot explain, and it would mean a fourth mechanism — which is worth
knowing too.

## Why this is not a diagnostic

`TODO.md`'s §6 has carried *"neither head checks `seq` continuity"* as a footnote
since the parity survey, and leticl assigns unconditionally as well. Tonight it was
promoted, and this is the paragraph that does it: a head can be a full turn behind
its own ledger with no indication, so **every** judgement made from that screen is
unsafe — the operator's about whether to send another prompt, and the head's about its
own session, which is the worse case of the two because the head cannot even see that
it is the one being misread.

## Tests

- `a_gap_in_the_log_is_said_repaired_and_counted` — a jump is counted, said **with the
  range**, and a resync is asked for; a second gap is a second line rather than the
  first restated; and **ten contiguous events say nothing at all**, which is the
  property that makes the line worth reading when it does appear.
- `a_body_with_no_row_and_a_daemon_that_is_ahead_are_both_counted` — the two numbers
  that are not `gaps`: an orphan body is counted, named, and drawn nowhere (asserted:
  the words are *not* in the conversation); an `Accepted` eight ahead sets `behind` to
  8, moves `gaps` not at all, and asks for no resync; a redelivery at this head's own
  seq is not a distance.
- `a_protocol_skew_is_said_at_the_handshake_and_the_head_stays_attached` — two frames
  raised from 60 to 72 rows, because `/status` gained three rows. The comment says so.

`cargo test -p letibot-tui`: **299 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored** (297 + these two).
`cargo test -p letibot-tui` all targets: 3 + 3 passed.

**`cargo check --workspace` does not currently run, and not because of this change**:
the sibling tree `/home/dead/Projects/rano` has an unclosed delimiter in
`src/syntax.rs` (`mod window_tests {` at 3801, still open at EOF) and `rano` is a path
dependency of this workspace. It is somebody's edit in flight, in another repository,
with its own dirty tree — left alone deliberately. `letibot-tui` linked and ran, which
is the crate this change is in.

## Not in this commit

**R16 is the same root cause and is next**: an echo retires on `TranscriptContent`
matched by text (R2's rule, and it stays — B's comment records that matching any other
way took the wrong prompt off first when two of different lengths were queued), but a
compaction *replaces the transcript*, so the row the echo was waiting for is summarised
away and the retiring event never arrives in the form it expects. The fix belongs where
a binding is *superseded*, not where it is confirmed.

**The three blocked store queries are written down** in
`~/Projects/blocked-queries-r11-r16.md`, with what each was for. They matter because
the R16 test needs one of them: a prompt whose row text is only ever a *piece* of a
longer row means the merged case in `retire_pending`'s own comment is live too, and
that is a second mechanism producing the same furniture.
